<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · disaster</p><h1 class="enc-title">1931 China floods</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1931">1931</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as 1931 Yangtze–Huai River floods, Yangtze-Huai Flood Disaster</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The 1931 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> floods were a series of floods from June to August 1931 along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> and Huai rivers, which struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuhan">Wuhan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> and other cities and culminated in a dike breach along Lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaoyou">Gaoyou</a> on 25 August. After a long drought and a harsh winter, snowmelt met heavy rain and nine cyclones in July, and the waters covered about 180,000 square kilometres. Estimates of the dead vary widely: a survey led by John Lossing Buck found 150,000 drowned, Chinese historians led by Li Wenhai counted 422,420, and some Western sources claim 3.7 to 4 million from famine and disease. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> government set up the National Flood Relief Commission under T.V. Soong.</p></div>
